queer people make history all the time  even if your teacher forgot to mention  that part  hello and welcome to an episode of  historical icons who weren't actually  straight today's icon is james baldwin  he was a civil rights era american  author writer and playwright who  illustrated the struggle of his fellow  black americans with a unique and  nuanced voice he championed love and  emotional connection as a powerful and  necessary force for change on top of all  of that he was queer  up first some fast facts  baldwin was born on august 2nd 1924 and  grew up in poverty in harlem new york  with his family his mom abusive  stepfather and eight half-siblings eight  i only have one and that's more than  enough his extraordinary gift for words  first shown as a 14 year old junior  minister and with stories and plays he  wrote for his high school dwight clinton  he later chronicled this period of  self-exploration through religion and  sexuality with his first novel go tell  it on the mountain by his mid-20s  baldwin fed up with america's  mistreatment of black people and wanting  to see himself outside of that context  secured a writing fellowship that  allowed him to move to  paris  baldwin bloomed creatively publishing  many of his most celebrated works like  giovanni's room and notes of a native  son a powerful essay collection on  racial inequality over time his french  riviera home became a kind of refuge for  other black revolutionary artists oh to  be a fly on that wall baldwin was also  close friends with many other  influential people of the time like  marlon brando maya angelou and ray  charles  in 1957 the civil rights movement was in  full force and baldwin returned to  america an incredible 1965 debate that  you should definitely go watch between  baldwin and william buckley shows  baldwin's virtuoso ability to paint in  clear words the painful realities of  being black in america saying it comes  as a great shock to discover that the  flag to which you have pledged  allegiance along with everybody else has  not pledged allegiance to you  following the assassinations of his  close friends and civil rights giants  medgar evers malcolm x and martin luther  king baldwin returned to france to  recover from his grief  he then wrote some of his most powerful  works shining a light on black love and  humanity by telling the stories of black  families his 1974 novel the  heartbreaking if beale street could talk  tells the story of a young black couple  whose dreams are torn apart by a false  rape accusation in a racist police  system baldwin's words contributed  immensely to the movement in their own  way as he put it we can disagree and  still love each other unless your  disagreement is rooted in my oppression  and denial of my humanity and right to  exist  now on to the quiz  baldwin dated men but he viewed his  sexual identity through a broad lens and  shouldn't we all he chose to view and  love people as themselves resisting  sexuality and gender-related labels  which he saw as pointlessly shifting  over time  one of baldwin's most significant  relationships was with a swiss painter  named lucian hackersberger they lived  together for a few months in a small  chalet in 1951 which honestly sounds  amazing  it was there that baldwin was able to  write uninterrupted and experience a  slice of domesticity with a partner for  the first time in his life although  their relationship ended and baldwin was  devastated they remained on good terms  never afraid to write about love in ways  that were socially unacceptable for the  times in 1951 baldwin wrote giovanni's  room dedicated to lucien deemed very  controversial it's centered around the  lives of gay white characters living in  paris  giovanni's room came to be a success and  helped make space for the representation  of gay life in literature he continued  to normalize queer relationships by  writing books that featured queer  characters like the best sellers another  country and just above my head during  the years that baldwin returned to  america to participate in the civil  rights movement he was prevented at  times from taking a more active role as  some religious leaders and black  activists because of his sexuality and  relative to them more tempered views  undeterred he continued to write and to  speak where he could unapologetically  expressing his own beliefs  baldwin died in 1987 of stomach cancer  with lucian at his bedside more than 30  years after their affair  in conclusion  james baldwin was a huge intellectual  influence on the civil rights movement  wrote enduring works of literature  normalized queer characters in his  writing and had a cute artsy boyfriend  throughout his life voldemort was  unflinching in his belief that people  who identified as lgbtq were worthy of  love and respect despite the outright  hostility he often encountered he is now  regarded as a hero to several trans  activists like janet mock because of his  advocacy for androgyny and non-binary  identities in his later writings  especially in his essays here be dragons  and to crush the serpent his writing and  message resonate with the ongoing battle  for equality today  if you haven't seen raul peck's  documentary i am not your negro based on  baldwin's unfinished final memoir or  barry jenkins academy award-winning  adaptation of if bill street could talk  put them on your weekend list  [Music]  thanks for watching and check out the  next episode of historic icons who  weren't actually straight right here
 

